[{"id":"lut-predict-vote","itemId":"look-up-table","role":"warmup","promptHtml":"Each column is one step. This LUT3 holds vote's truth table: <code class=\"nobreak\">y</code> is 1 when at least two of <code class=\"nobreak\">a</code>, <code class=\"nobreak\">b</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">c</code> are 1. Steps 0 and 1 are drawn for you. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">y</code> for the rest.","explanationHtml":"<p>In each step the three inputs pick one row of the truth table, and the LUT puts out the bit stored there. Count the 1s among <code class=\"nobreak\">a</code>, <code class=\"nobreak\">b</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">c</code>: two or three give 1, none or one give 0. In the simulation <code class=\"nobreak\">y</code> changes in the same step as the inputs, because nothing in a LUT waits.</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"vote-mixed","source":"hdl/level-00/vote/vote_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"step","cycles":8,"signals":[{"label":"a","path":"vote_tb.a","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[1,"1"],[2,"0"],[3,"1"],[5,"0"],[6,"1"],[7,"0"]]},{"label":"b","path":"vote_tb.b","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[1,"1"],[3,"0"],[4,"1"],[5,"0"],[7,"1"]]},{"label":"c","path":"vote_tb.c","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[2,"1"],[3,"0"],[4,"1"],[7,"0"]]},{"label":"y","path":"vote_tb.y","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[1,"1"],[3,"0"],[4,"1"],[5,"0"],[6,"1"],[7,"0"]],"samples":["0","1","1","0","1","0","1","0"]}]},"answer":"y","prefill":[0,1]},{"id":"lut-order-lookup","itemId":"look-up-table","role":"core","promptHtml":"Put these in the order they happen in vote's LUT.","explanationHtml":"<p>A LUT does not work anything out when its inputs change. Its eight bits, one for each row of vote's truth table, were stored when the design was loaded. After that, each new set of inputs only picks a row, and the bit already stored there becomes the output. That is why one LUT can hold any function of its inputs: a different design only stores different bits.</p>","type":"order","steps":["The design is loaded into the chip, and the LUT's eight bits are stored in it.","`a`, `b` and `c` take new values.","Together they pick one of the eight rows.","The LUT reads the bit stored in that row.","That bit appears on `y`."],"shuffled":[2,3,4,0,1]},{"id":"lut-predict-odd","itemId":"look-up-table","role":"stretch","promptHtml":"This LUT3 holds the truth table of an odd count: <code class=\"nobreak\">y</code> is 1 when an odd number of <code class=\"nobreak\">a</code>, <code class=\"nobreak\">b</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">c</code> are 1, that is one of them or all three. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">y</code> for every step.","explanationHtml":"<p>Count the 1s in each step: one or three give 1, none or two give 0. This rule has nothing in common with vote's, yet it fits the same kind of cell, one LUT3, with different bits stored in its eight rows. A LUT holds whatever bit each row needs, so any function of its inputs fits.</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"odd3-rows","source":"hdl/level-00/odd3/odd3_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"step","cycles":8,"signals":[{"label":"a","path":"odd3_tb.a","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[1,"1"],[2,"0"],[3,"1"],[4,"0"],[6,"1"]]},{"label":"b","path":"odd3_tb.b","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"1"],[2,"0"],[3,"1"],[5,"0"]]},{"label":"c","path":"odd3_tb.c","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[3,"1"],[7,"0"]]},{"label":"y","path":"odd3_tb.y","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"1"],[1,"0"],[3,"1"],[4,"0"],[5,"1"],[6,"0"],[7,"1"]],"samples":["1","0","0","1","0","1","0","1"]}]},"answer":"y"},{"id":"lut-fill-pair","itemId":"look-up-table","role":"core","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">y</code> should be 1 when at least two of <code class=\"nobreak\">a</code>, <code class=\"nobreak\">b</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">c</code> are 1. One pair is missing from vote's line. Type it, using <code class=\"nobreak\">&amp;</code> for and.","explanationHtml":"<p>At least two of three inputs are 1 exactly when some pair of them is: <code class=\"nobreak\">a</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">b</code>, or <code class=\"nobreak\">a</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">c</code>, or <code class=\"nobreak\">b</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">c</code>. The line already has the first and the last pair, joined by <code class=\"nobreak\">|</code> (or), so the missing one is <code>a &amp; c</code>. However you write that pair, with or without brackets and in either order, Vivado turns the line into the same eight stored bits of one LUT3 (lesson 0.1).</p>","type":"fill","file":"hdl/level-00/vote/vote.sv","firstLine":1,"totalLines":9,"before":["// y is 1 when at least two of a, b and c are 1: a vote of three.","module vote (","  input  logic a,","  input  logic b,","  input  logic c,","  output logic y",");"],"blankLine":{"left":"  assign y = (a & b) | ","right":" | (b & c);"},"after":["endmodule"],"highlighted":{"before":[[{"text":"// y is 1 when at least two of a, b and c are 1: a vote of three.","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"module","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"vote","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":" (","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" a,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" b,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" c,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"output","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" y","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":");","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}]],"blankLine":{"left":[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"assign","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" y ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" (a ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"&","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" b) ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"|","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],"right":[{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"|","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" (b ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"&","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" c);","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}]},"after":[[{"text":"endmodule","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true}]]},"accept":["(a & c)","(c & a)","a & c","c & a"],"caseSensitive":true,"blankLabel":"The missing pair"},{"id":"lut-report-available","itemId":"look-up-table","role":"core","promptHtml":"Vivado built <code class=\"nobreak\">vote.sv</code> for the Arty A7-100T. Below is the first table of its Utilization report. A slice is one of the small groups of LUTs and flip-flops the chip is built from, so the Slice LUTs row counts LUTs. Used is how many this design takes; Available is how many the whole chip has.","explanationHtml":"<p>Read the Available column of the Slice LUTs row: that is every LUT on this chip. The Used column beside it is vote's share, and Util% is that share as a percentage. A bigger design uses more of them; the chip itself never changes.</p>","type":"report","source":"vivado/reports/vote/arty-a7-100t/utilization.rpt","firstLine":32,"excerpt":["+-------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","|        Site Type        | Used | Fixed | Prohibited | Available | Util% |","+-------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+","| Slice LUTs              |    1 |     0 |          0 |     63400 | <0.01 |","|   LUT as Logic          |    1 |     0 |          0 |     63400 | <0.01 |","|   LUT as Memory         |    0 |     0 |          0 |     19000 |  0.00 |","| Slice Registers         |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","|   Register as Flip Flop |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","|   Register as Latch     |    0 |     0 |          0 |    126800 |  0.00 |","| F7 Muxes                |    0 |     0 |          0 |     31700 |  0.00 |","| F8 Muxes                |    0 |     0 |          0 |     15850 |  0.00 |","| Unique Control Sets     |    0 |       |          0 |     15850 |  0.00 |","+-------------------------+------+-------+------------+-----------+-------+"],"ask":"How many LUTs does this FPGA have in all?","answer":"63400","numeric":true}]